    "Still have the cardboard box the air fryer came in, nine months later, folded flat behind the dresser, because some part of me believes I will return the air fryer.\n\nI will not return the air fryer. The window closed in February. I use it four times a week. And yet the box stays, because throwing out the box is the moment I admit this is permanent, and I am not prepared to be a person who has permanently chosen an air fryer.\n\nThere are six boxes back there. Six. A monument to reversibility, to the possibility that any of this could be undone and returned to the shelf it came from, original packaging intact, no questions asked. The dresser box is from 2018. I love that box.": "2026-11-03",
    "Rubber ducks are the last witnesses in most houses.": "2026-11-05",
    "Discovered the cutting board has a second side. Both sides are identical. I have used the same side exclusively for roughly five years, and there is a pristine, untouched, structurally equivalent surface facing the counter at all times.\n\nI flipped it today and it felt enormously wrong, like sitting in someone else's chair in a class with no assigned seating. The new side is smooth. It is the board as the manufacturer intended, preserved perfectly through nothing but my own unexamined habit.\n\nI flipped it back after about ten minutes. I'm not saving it for anything. There's no occasion for which a fresh cutting board side is appropriate. But I'd rather know it's there than spend it, which I suspect is true of several other things in my life that I'm not going to list in an email.": "2026-11-07",
    "The extension ladder is in the garage fully extended and has been since August, because I extended it once and now I cannot convince it to come back down. The mechanism is a hook. The hook has opinions.\n\nHere is what nobody tells you when you buy an extension ladder: extension is a one-way verb in practice. Everything that extends wants to stay extended. Bridges, deadlines, arguments at Thanksgiving. The ladder leaned itself diagonally across the entire garage and now I park on the street like a visitor at my own house.\n\nI have looked up videos. The men in the videos collapse the ladder with one hand while talking. They have a relationship with it I will never have. I've started referring to it as a feature of the room.": "2026-11-09",
    "I wish to formally document the status of the doorstop in the break room, which is a rubber wedge that has not touched a door in my entire tenure here.\n\nIt lives six inches from the hinge side, which is the wrong side. Someone placed it there, presumably in error, presumably years ago, and every person since has stepped over it rather than move it four feet. The door swings freely. The doorstop observes.\n\nI would characterize this as the central operational metaphor of our department, but I am aware that characterizing things is not in my job description, and the last time I characterized something it ended up in a performance review. So: rubber wedge, break room, wrong side, no action required. Logged.": "2026-11-12",
    "Consult the hamster before signing anything.": "2026-11-16",
    "You bought a snow shovel in a city without winter.": "2026-11-18",
    "Please be advised that the drop ceiling in the east corridor has a tile that is approximately one-half inch out of alignment, and I have raised this through the appropriate channel twice.\n\nThe relevant question, which Facilities has declined to engage with, is what the ceiling is dropping from. A drop ceiling implies an original ceiling above it, a true ceiling, hidden, retained for reasons. We have built a false sky in this building and then named it honestly and then stopped thinking about it.\n\nI pushed the tile up with a mop handle on Tuesday. There is space up there. Considerable space. Pipes, and then dark, and then presumably the real ceiling, which has not seen a human being since 1987 and has nonetheless been holding everything up the entire time without acknowledgment.": "2026-11-19",
    "Cleaned the lint trap and I need to say that the lint is the sweater. Not evidence of the sweater. Not a byproduct. It's the actual sweater, incrementally, leaving in gray handfuls over the course of several years.\n\nEvery wash takes a little. We call it lint because calling it \"a thin layer of the thing you own\" would make laundry unbearable. I held it up to the light and I could see the blue from the towels and the maroon from that shirt I like, all of it pressed into one sheet, a felted archive of attrition.\n\nI threw it away like a normal person. Then I stood there for a while. Then I did the second load, which is what you do.": "2026-11-20",
    "Sign the waiver using someone else's posture.": "2026-11-22",
    "Replying all to this because the group email has reached the stage where nobody remembers the original request and we are now just confirming receipt of each other's confirmations.\n\nThere are forty-one of us. I counted. Thirty-eight have no conceivable stake in the outcome, including a man in Logistics who left in March and whose address apparently still resolves. He has not replied. He is the wisest of us.\n\nI keep thinking about the first message. It asked something small. A date, maybe. That question is now buried under nine layers of \"thanks!\" and two out-of-office autoreplies that triggered each other for an hour on Sunday. Nobody will answer it. The thread will outlive the project it was created to serve, and at some point we'll all just quietly stop, the way a field goes dark.": "2026-11-23",
    "Your name is on the waiver twice and in the margin once.": "2026-11-24",
    "So I own one bookend. One. A bookend is the only object on earth that is useless in the singular and I have been pretending otherwise since 2016.\n\nIt's a heavy brass thing shaped like a horse's head and it currently holds up the left side of nine cookbooks whose right side is held up by a jar of dried chickpeas. Everyone who comes into my kitchen sees this. Nobody has ever said anything, which I think reveals something unflattering about the social contract.\n\nThe missing one is somewhere. It had a partner. Somewhere there is half a horse in a box in a storage unit in a town I no longer live in, holding nothing, doing its job perfectly in total darkness against no books whatsoever.": "2026-11-26",
    "Frost on the windshield this morning in a pattern I can only describe as deliberate, feathered, branching like something that had a plan and executed it overnight while I slept forty feet away.\n\nI scraped it off. That's the job. Thirty seconds of destroying the single most beautiful object I would encounter all day so I could drive to a building and look at a spreadsheet. The scraper made that sound. You know the sound.\n\nHere's the thing: nobody photographs windshield frost. We photograph sunsets, which happen constantly and require nothing of us. Frost is a one-night engagement that performs exclusively for the first person outside and is contractually obligated to be ruined by them. Lovely stuff. Cheers.": "2026-11-27"
